You can also enable inference tables on an existing endpoint using the API. After inference tables are enabled, continue specifying the same auto_capture_config body in future update endpoint API calls to continue using inference tables.  
Note  
Changing the table location after enabling inference tables is not supported.  
PUT /api/2.0/serving-endpoints/{name}/config { "served_entities": [ { "name":"current", "entity_name":"model-A", "entity_version":"1", "workload_size":"Small", "scale_to_zero_enabled":true }, { "name":"challenger", "entity_name":"model-B", "entity_version":"1", "workload_size":"Small", "scale_to_zero_enabled":true } ], "traffic_config":{ "routes": [ { "served_model_name":"current", "traffic_percentage":"50" }, { "served_model_name":"challenger", "traffic_percentage":"50" } ] }, "auto_capture_config":{ "catalog_name": "catalog", "schema_name": "schema", "table_name_prefix": "my-endpoint" } }

Disable inference tables
Disable inference tables
When disabling inference tables, you do not need to specify catalog, schema, or table prefix. The only required field is enabled: false.  
POST /api/2.0/serving-endpoints { "name": "feed-ads", "config":{ "served_entities": [ { "entity_name": "ads1", "entity_version": "1", "workload_size": "Small", "scale_to_zero_enabled": true } ], "auto_capture_config":{ "enabled": false } } }  
To re-enable a disabled inference table follow the instructions in Enable inference tables on an existing endpoint. You can use either the same table or specify a new table.

Next steps
Next steps
After you enable inference tables, you can monitor the served models in your model serving endpoint with Databricks Lakehouse Monitoring. For details, see Workflow: Monitor model performance using inference tables.. Based on this, answer the following question: How can I enable inference tables on an existing Databricks model serving endpoint using the API?"},{"role":"assistant","content":"To enable inference tables on an existing Databricks model serving endpoint using the API, you can use the PUT method with the `/api/2.0/serving-endpoints/{name}/config` endpoint. Here's an example request body:
```json
{
  "served_entities": [
    {
      "name":"current",
      "entity_name":"model-A",
      "entity_version":"1",
      "workload_size":"Small",
      "scale_to_zero_enabled":true
    },
    {
      "name":"challenger",
      "entity_name":"model-B",
      "entity_version":"1",
      "workload_size":"Small",
      "scale_to_zero_enabled":true
    }
  ],
  "traffic_config":{
    "routes": [
      {
        "served_model_name":"current",
        "traffic_percentage":"50"
      },
      {
        "served_model_name":"challenger",
        "traffic_percentage":"50"
      }
    ]
  },
  "auto_capture_config":{
    "catalog_name": "catalog",
    "schema_name": "schema",
    "table_name_prefix": "my-endpoint"
  }
}
```
In the `auto_capture_config` section, specify the `catalog_name`, `schema_name`, and `table_name_prefix` to enable inference tables. Note that changing the table location after enabling inference tables is not supported.

To disable inference tables, use the POST method with the `/api/2.0/serving-endpoints` endpoint and set `enabled` to `false` in the `auto_capture_config` section. Here's an example request body:
```json
{
  "name": "feed-ads",
  "config":{
    "served_entities": [
      {
        "entity_name": "ads1",
        "entity_version": "1",
        "workload_size": "Small",
        "scale_to_zero_enabled": true
      }
    ],
    "auto_capture_config":{
      "enabled": false
    }
  }
}
```
To re-enable a disabled inference table, follow the instructions in Enable inference tables on an existing endpoint. You can use either the same table or specify a new table."}]

