1. Create a file named `job.tf` and add the following content to the file:
```hcl
variable "job_name" {
  description = "A name for the job."
  type        = string
  default     = "My Job"
}

variable "task_key" {
  description = "A name for the task."
  type        = string
  default     = "my_task"
}

resource "databricks_job" "this" {
  name = var.job_name

  task {
    task_key          = var.task_key
    existing_cluster_id = databricks_cluster.this.cluster_id
    notebook_task {
      notebook_path = databricks_notebook.this.path
    }
  }

  email_notifications {
    on_success = [data.databricks_current_user.me.user_name]
    on_failure = [data.databricks_current_user.me.user_name]
  }
}

output "job_url" {
  value = databricks_job.this.url
}
```
2. Create another file named `job.auto.tfvars` and add the following content to the file:
```makefile
job_name = "My Job"
task_key = "my_task"
```
This file contains a variable value for customizing the job configuration.

3. Make sure you have the "Run your first ETL workload on Databricks" Python notebook in your Databricks workspace. If not, you can create it by referring to the documentation provided.

4. Run `terraform apply` to create the job. Once the job is created, you can use the `job_url` output to access the job in your Databricks workspace.

Note: This assumes you have Terraform and the Databricks provider installed and configured in your environment. If not, you will need to set those up first."}]

